 "youtube": {
  "title": "Tulip Mania: Did a Flower Really Ruin Holland?",
  "description": "Tulip mania is the most famous crash in history. In the winter of 1636–37, a single tulip bulb could cost as much as a house in the Dutch Republic, and bulbs still in the frozen ground were traded on paper in taverns. Then, in February, the buyers vanished. The legend of ruined merchants was made famous by Charles Mackay in 1841, but historian Anne Goldgar searched the archives and found almost no one ruined by it.\n\nSubscribe to Stickstory for a true story like this every day. Would you have bought a tulip bulb in 1637? Tell us in the comments!\n\n#shorts #tulipmania #stickstory",
